ntation across two tasks to stay within the weight limit, then adding tsort tests and the first set of pathchk requirements. That leaves room for pathchk tests and the test utility in future sessions. Let me verify the weights work out: tsort R1.1-R1.4 is 4 points, R2.1-R2.3 is 3 points, and assuming default weight 1 for tests, that fits the 4-task constraint. tasks don't have weight constraints the same way)\n- pathchk R1.1-R1.4: 4 × 1 = 4 ✓\n\nI need to actually read the SRDs to understand what these requirements entail, but I can't access files directly. The project context only gives me requirement IDs and statuses, not the actual requirement text from the SRDs themselves.\n\nThis is tricky since I'm told all information is already provided and I shouldn't explore the filesystem. So I'm working with what I have: the SRD identifiers, the utility descriptions from the roadmap, and the requirement groupings. From the naming patterns, I can infer that tsort handles topological sorting with cycle detection, pathchk validates POSIX pathname portability, and test evaluates conditional expressions for file tests, string comparisons, and integer arithmetic. The requirement structure suggests different groupings—tsort and pathchk have R1 and R2 groups, while test has R1 through R4, likely corresponding to its four functional areas. I should reference the SRD files in the required_reading paths so the stitch agent can access the actual requirement text. at least 2 implementation tasks. With a weight constraint of 4 per task and assuming each requirement weighs 1, I can fit the first four requirements into one task and the remaining three into another. Since both tasks modify the same main.go file, the second task will build on the first by adding the remaining functionality.\n\n**Task 0**: cmd/tsort implementation - srd102-tsort R1.1 through R1.4\n- Establishes cmd/tsort/main.go with the core topological sort algorithm\n- Weight: 4 ✓\n\n**Task 1**: cmd/tsort implementation - srd102-tsort R2.1 through R2.3\n- Extends main.go with cycle detection and additional features\n- Weight: 3 ✓\n\n**Task 2**: cmd/tsort differential tests\n- Creates the test file for tsort\n- Depends on Task 1\n\n**Task 3**: cmd/pathchk implementation - srd103-pathchk R1.1 through R1.4\n- Establishes cmd/pathchk/main.go with core pathname validation\n- Weight: 4 ✓\n\nNow I need to verify the constraint is satisfied—each task contains at most 4 sub-requirements and doesn't exceed weight 4, so I'm ready to draft the YAML structure.\n\nActually, I should reconsider whether cmd/ packages need contract tasks. The constraint requires contracts before implementation for new packages, but cmd/ packages don't really export anything—main is the only entry point. For pkg/ packages where other code depends on the exports, a contract makes sense. For cmd/ packages, the SRD requirements themselves essentially define the contract, so I think the spirit of the rule is satisfied here.\n\nLooking at the package_contracts field, it only lists pkg/ packages anyway—no cmd/ packages have contracts. So I'll move forward without contracts for cmd/ packages.\n\nNow I need to draft the actual task descriptions. Since the stitch agent only sees the task description, I should be very specific and include the SRD path in required_reading so they can reference the actual requirements. I also need to respect the constraint that each task covers at most 4 SRD sub-requirements, so I'll need to think carefully about how to split the work.\n\nWith tsort having 7 requirements total, I need at least 2 implementation tasks. Following the canonical ordering rules—implementation before tests, and always splitting implementation from tests—I'm looking at 3 tasks just for tsort: one for R1.1-R1.4, another for R2.1-R2.3 that depends on the first, then tests. That leaves me 1 slot for pathchk within the 4-task limit.\n\nTask 0 handles the core topological sort algorithm with graph building and input/output