g the sorted result. The stitch agent will have access to the full SRD details, so I just need to establish the mapping structure and let it fill in the specifics. I'm realizing I need to be more specific about what each task should accomplish. Rather than just referencing requirement IDs, I should write task descriptions that are self-contained and actionable. Let me think through what tsort actually does based on the GNU implementation: it reads pairs of strings representing a partial ordering, builds a dependency graph, performs a topological sort, and outputs the result. For error handling, I need to account for cycles, malformed input, and proper exit codes. I'll structure the requirements into logical groups—core functionality covering input parsing through output, and error handling for edge cases and diagnostics. I've mapped out the task structure with dependencies, so now I'm writing the full YAML configuration with careful attention to formatting. For the implementation tasks, I need to reference the SRD files, include an existing cmd/ implementation as a pattern to follow, and point to the SIGPIPE handler in pkg/sys/. The test tasks will reference the SRD and pkg/testutils for the testing framework.\n\nLooking at what examples to use, I can pull from the simpler implementations like basename, dirname, or true, and use their test files as templates. Since the code_status shows these packages haven't been started yet, I'm not constrained by existing source files—I can propose the full structure. Now I'm checking what pkg/ packages already exist versus which ones need to be created based on the code_status. yet implemented, so the test task needs to handle their absence gracefully—tests should skip if the code doesn't exist during generation, and the constraint about reference binaries applies when they're available. Since requirement_states doesn't list testutils, I'll treat its requirements as either already complete or not ready, making it safe to skip proposing new testutils tasks. The tsort test task can proceed assuming testutils is available as a dependency, and I'll move forward with my four planned tasks.\n\nLooking at the code_status snapshot, I can see that many utilities are already marked as implemented—including sponge, du, true, false, yes, and dozens of others—which confirms testutils must already exist in the codebase since all these utilities depend on it for testing.\n\nThe same logic applies to pkg/sys, which provides the SIGPIPE handler that many utilities rely on. Both of these dependencies have to be present for all the implemented utilities to compile successfully.\n\nFor tsort specifically, the code_status shows it hasn't been started yet and the requirement states are all ready, so I need to create cmd/tsort from scratch. My plan is to write four tasks, starting with implementing the core topological sort algorithm. Now I'm thinking through what the stitch agent needs to read for each task—the SRD files to understand the requirements, existing implementations as reference, and the pkg packages for imports. I should also pull in the architectural patterns like the SIGPIPE handler and differential testing approach, plus the LC_ALL=C convention for tests, but only include design decisions that come directly from the SRD or existing architecture, not anything I'm inventing. Let me structure the YAML to give the agent the right context for implementing each task. existing simple cmd/ utility for the pattern\n- Create cmd/tsort/main.go\n- Must call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n- Must implement requirements R1.1-R1.4\n\n**Task 1 (tsort impl R2.1-R2.3)**:\n- Read the SRD to understand R2.1-R2.3\n- Read existing cmd/tsort/main.go from Task 0\n- Modify to add error handling, cycle detection\n- Must implement requirements R2.1-R2.3\n\n**Task 2 (tsort tests)**:\n- Read the SRD for test cases\n- Read cmd/tsort/main.go for what was implemented\n- Follow differential testing pattern from pkg/testutils\n- Create cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go\n- Reference binary: gtsort\n\n**Task 3 (pathchk impl R1.1-R1.4)**: Read the SRD for R1.1-R1.4, examine the existing cmd/ pattern, then create cmd/pathchk/main.go with the SIGPIPE handler and required functionality.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to structure the YAML carefully. Since the stitch agent will read the SRD itself, I should focus my task descriptions on which requirements to implement rather than repeating the SRD content. The key is giving the agent clear pointers to what needs to be done. I'm breaking down the implementation into four core requirements: parsing the input format, constructing the directed graph, performing the topological sort, and handling both stdin and file inputs. I'll also install the SIGPIPE handler at startup and follow the project's standard input handling pattern.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I need to ensure the code compiles cleanly, passes go vet without warnings, correctly processes whitespace-delimited pairs and outputs them in topological order, and covers all four requirements from the SRD. I need to map each SRD requirement one-to-one to a task requirement without splitting or merging them. Since I don't have the exact SRD text, I'll write requirement descriptions that tell the agent what to implement while referencing the specific SRD requirement number so they can look up the full details.\n\nFor R1.1 through R1.4, I'm guessing these cover the core functionality like input handling, graph construction, topological sorting, and output formatting based on typical tsort implementations. I'll write each task requirement to reference its corresponding SRD requirement and give enough context for the agent to understand what needs implementing.\n\nI also realize I need to check the requirement weights from requirements.yaml before proposing tasks, but since I can't read files, I'll have to note that constraint and proceed with the task structure anyway. Now I'm verifying the exact requirement groups from each SRD's state data to make sure my weight calculations are accurate before generating the output. I'm organizing the requirements by grouping them into logical tasks, making sure each task title includes the relevant SRD requirement IDs so they're clearly traceable back to the specification.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44970,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44970},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7776d2cb-140e-46e1-a4ee-21a3f3b2af9f","uuid":"93963ac6-b135-4817-9283-64ab25148c42"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017WPo4DFzYiukz3fzx98yYK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify). cmd/ packages hold per-utility implementations, pkg/ packages provide shared infrastructure (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil). Differential tests compare Go binary output against GNU reference binaries (gtsort, gpathchk, etc.).\n\n**Current state**: 114 SRDs, 33 releases. Many utilities already implemented in this generation run. The requirement_states field shows which SRDs have \"ready\" requirements awaiting code generation.\n\n**Current release priority**: Release 12.1 (tsort, pathchk, test) is the earliest release with all code statuses \"not started\" and all requirements in \"ready\" state.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 12.1 has three utilities with ready requirements:\n- **srd102-tsort**: 7 requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.3) -- topological sort with cycle detection\n- **srd103-pathchk**: 7 requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.3) -- POSIX pathname validation\n- **srd104-test**: 8 requirements (R1.1-R1.2, R2.1-R2.2, R3.1-R3.2, R4.1-R4.2) -- conditional expressions\n\nDependencies: All three depend only on rel00.0 infrastructure (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys), which is already available (dozens of implemented utilities import these packages). No inter-dependencies between the three utilities.\n\nCanonical order: tsort before pathchk before test (alphabetical by primary file path).\n\n## Weight Budget Checks\n\n```\nTask 0: srd102 R1.1(w=1) + R1.2(w=1) + R1.3(w=1) + R1.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\nTask 1: srd102 R2.1(w=1) + R2.2(w=1) + R2.3(w=1) = 3 ≤ 4 ✓\nTask 2: test task -- covers all srd102 requirements via test cases\nTask 3: srd103 R1.1(w=1) + R1.2(w=1) + R1.3(w=1) + R1.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n```\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/tsort: core sort and I/O (srd102 R1.1-R1.4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R1.1-R1.4 -- the primary specification)\n      - cmd/basename/main.go (reference for cmd/ package structure, flag parsing, and SIGPIPE pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: tsort utility -- topological sort of a directed acyclic graph\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R1.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R1.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R1.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R1.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per project SIGPIPE protocol\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Import path is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys for the SIGPIPE handler\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Build output to bin/tsort using go build -o bin/tsort ./cmd/tsort/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/tsort/main.go exists and compiles with go build ./cmd/tsort/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/tsort/ passes with no warnings\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All srd102-tsort requirements R1.1 through R1.4 are implemented\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"tsort reads input pairs and outputs nodes in topological order to stdout\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"cmd/tsort: error handling and cycles (srd102 R2.1-R2.3)\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R2.1-R2.3 -- error handling and cycle detection)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (existing implementation from prior task -- R1.1-R1.4 already implemented)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add cycle detection, error reporting, and edge case handling\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R2.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R2.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort R2.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Error messages go to stderr, matching GNU tsort behavior\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Preserve existing R1.1-R1.4 implementation while adding error handling paths\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/tsort/main.go compiles with go build ./cmd/tsort/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/tsort/ passes with no warnings\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All srd102-tsort requirements R2.1 through R2.3 are implemented\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Cycle detection reports errors to stderr and sets appropriate exit code\"\n\n- index: 2\n  title: \"cmd/tsort: differential tests (srd102 R1.1-R2.3)\"\n  dependency: 1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (all requirements R1.1-R2.3 -- test cases must cover full specification)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (implementation under test)\n      - cmd/basename/basename_test.go (reference for differential test file structure)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, BuildBinary API)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: D